    {
      "title": "Spain begins mourning period as search continues for train crash victims",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/29/spain-mourning-period-deadly-train-crash",
      "description": "<p>Emergency services, drones and dog unit continue to comb carriages as Civil Guard sets up clinics for relatives to provide DNA samples</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/29/how-did-the-spain-train-crash-happen-what-will-investigators-be-asking\">How did the Spain train crash happen and what will investigators be asking?</a></p></li></ul><p>Spain began three days of mourning on Thursday for at least 69 people who died in a high-speed rail collision, as hospitals continued to treat dozens of injured passengers and train drivers went on strike to urge more investment in railway safety.</p><p>The trains – one from Spain’s state-owned Renfe and the other from the private rail company Iryo – were travelling from Madrid towards the southern region of Andalucía on Wednesday when they collided just before 9am. After the Iryo train came to a halt, the other slammed into its rear, knocking several carriages off the track near Urda in the central region of Castilla-La Mancha.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/29/spain-mourning-period-deadly-train-crash\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-29T17:59:03"
    },
